<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) enforce safety alignment to reliably refuse malicious requests, yet the same blanket safeguards also block legitimate uses in policing, defense, and other high-stakes settings. Earlier “refusal-direction” edits can bypass those layers, but they rely on a single vector that indiscriminately unlocks all hazardous topics, offering no semantic control. We introduce Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors (MEUV), a lightweight framework that factorizes the monolithic refusal direction into topic-aligned, nearly orthogonal vectors, each dedicated to one sensitive capability. MEUV is learned in a single epoch with a multi-task objective that blends a differential-ablation margin, cross-topic and orthogonality penalties, and several auxiliary terms. On bilingual malicious-prompt benchmarks, MEUV achieves an attack success rate of no less than 87% on Gemma-2-2B, LLaMA-3-8B, and Qwen-7B, yet cuts cross-topic leakage by up to 90% compared with the best single-direction baseline. Vectors trained in Chinese transfer almost unchanged to English (and vice versa), suggesting a language-agnostic refusal subspace. The results show that fine-grained, topic-level capability activation is achievable with minimal utility loss, paving the way for controlled LLMs deployment in security-sensitive domains.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">K</em><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eywords</span> Large Language Models  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Safety Alignment  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Refusal Behaviors  <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Capability Unlocking</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) now rival human performance in open-ended
generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Widespread deployment, however, is
constrained by global safety alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> that forces the model to refuse entire classes of requests, including those that are legitimate in
security-sensitive settings such as policing or intelligence analysis.
Geometric studies of these layers have converged on two views:
(i) a <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> latent direction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> whose removal disables most refusal
behavior, and (ii) a higher-dimensional <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refusal cone</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> that covers a
broader set of unsafe concepts. Both mechanisms are coarse: a single direction over-generalities, whereas a cone still lacks semantic resolution (as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Therefore, these methods are unable to grant, for example, narcotics analysts access to drug-related directives while still restricting access to terrorism-related content.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12221v1/method_cmp.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/293;" width="476" height="293" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Comparison of the MEUV framework with existing LLMs capability unlocking methods. The single-vector-based method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> assumes that the rejection behavior of LLMs is controlled by a single vector, whereas the concept-cone-based method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> regulates the model’s global rejection behavior through a multi-dimensional cone. In contrast, our proposed MEUV framework suggests that the unlocking vector of an LLMs can be decomposed into semantically exclusive sub-vectors, enabling more fine-grained capability unlocking.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We posit that the refusal manifold can be semantically factorized
into a small set of near-orthogonal vectors, each tied to one hazardous
capability. Activating a vector should unlock its target topic while
leaving all others intact. To realize this idea, we introduce
<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors (MEUV)</span>, trained by a lightweight
multi-task objective that marries a differential-ablation margin, a
cross-topic penalty, an orthogonality regularizer, and several auxiliary
terms for utility preservation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our contributions are three-fold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.6em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Problem Formalization and Theoretical Guarantee.</em> We formulate fine-grained capability activation as a constrained optimization problem and prove that our proposed relaxed objective can provide an upper bound on cross-topic leakage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pluggable Security Layer.</em> We design the first semantic mutual exclusion vector editor, which, using only synthetic data and a frozen backbone model, is able to learn vectors that are nearly orthogonal and aligned with specific topics. Additionally, we introduce a contrastive router as a semantic gate, forming a dual-layer security barrier. This framework is hot-pluggable during inference and does not require any modification to model weights.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Empirics.</em> On balanced Chinese and English malicious-prompt
benchmarks, MEUV attains <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\!87\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>87</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\!87\%</annotation></semantics></math> attack-success rate on
Gemma-2-2B, LLaMA-3-8B, and Qwen-7B, while cutting cross-topic
leakage by up to 90 % versus the strongest single-direction
baseline. Vectors learned in one language transfer almost
unchanged to the other, suggesting a language-agnostic refusal
subspace.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">MEUV demonstrates that the security layer of large language models can be selectively disabled with minimal impact on model utility, enabling controllable deployment in high-risk scenarios where a blanket denial approach is impractical.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Safety Alignment of LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the rapid expansion of model parameter scales and the improvement of task generalization capabilities, LLMs are increasingly prone to generating inaccurate, biased, or even harmful content. Consequently, the research community has summarized the objective of safety alignment as ensuring that models remain "helpful," "harmless," and "honest," commonly referred to as the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HHH</em> principle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) framework proposed by OpenAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> was the first to systematically demonstrate that aligned small- and medium-sized models can outperform larger unaligned models in terms of factuality and toxicity mitigation, thereby establishing a technical baseline for subsequent research. Following this, a multitude of methods aimed at improving safety alignment have emerged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, significantly enhancing the safety and usability of models in multi-task scenarios. In tandem, corresponding evaluation methodologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and benchmark datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> have been gradually refined, providing standardized metrics for quantifying alignment effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, a series of studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> have shown that alignment may also introduce unintended side effects: when user queries contain sensitive words such as "kill," the model may trigger excessive refusals even in benign contexts (e.g., "How do I kill a Python process?"), thereby undermining practicality and limiting applicability in high-sensitivity domains such as law enforcement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and the military.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Jailbreak Attacks Against LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As alignment techniques have gradually matured, attackers have shifted to “zero-access” methods such as jailbreaking and prompt injection, aiming to directly hijack model behavior during the inference phase. Based on whether the attacker has access to the model’s parameters or gradients, such attacks can be categorized into black-box jailbreaks (input-output access only) and white-box jailbreaks (access to internal gradients or weight information).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For black-box attacks, the attacker treats the model as a “black box,” relying solely on prompt engineering and corpus injection at the language level to hijack model behavior. Common approaches include direct prompt injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, indirect prompt injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, token perturbation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and multi-turn chained induction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Since these methods do not require knowledge of model internals, they pose a direct threat to closed-source APIs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When attackers can access model weights or gradients, they can employ optimization algorithms to automatically search for efficient jailbreak suffixes, thus executing white-box jailbreak attacks. Representative works include the gradient-based GCG algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and the AutoDAN method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which combines gradient optimization with controllable generation. Although white-box approaches currently target mainly open-source models, the universal suffixes they produce often transfer to closed-source systems, thereby amplifying security risks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Jailbreak attacks—black-box prompt injections or white-box suffix searches—seek maximum bypass, indiscriminately revealing every restricted topic once triggered; they are brittle to decoding details and policy updates, and their plaintext tricks cannot be access-controlled. In contrast, pluggable unlock vectors furnish selective, auditable, and revocable control: each vector is bound to a single capability, lives server-side behind policy gates, and can be inserted or removed at run time without retraining the backbone. Because the intervention is a fixed linear edit, its effect is deterministic across sampling seeds and model upgrades, giving a level of stability and operational flexibility that text-level jailbreaks inherently lack.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Analysis of Refusal Behaviors and Capability Unlocking in LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety alignment does not hard-code “answer/refuse” decisions into the entire network; rather, studies by Wei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> demonstrate that refusal capabilities are highly concentrated in a small subset of parameters that can be manipulated through representational engineering. Pruning or applying minor low-rank modifications to these units can effectively dismantle the refusal mechanism with minimal loss of general performance, highlighting the existence of a so-called “brittle layer.” Furthermore, Arditi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> found that in many models, this mechanism can be approximately controlled by a single directional vector—by simply adding or removing a one-dimensional subspace in the residual stream, the refusal functionality can be toggled on or off. Most recently, Wollschläger et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> introduced the “concept cone” model, suggesting that refusal decisions are actually determined by a cluster of independent yet composable vectors, which provide separable geometric switches for different safety protocols. While these approaches enable the one-time “unlocking” of a model’s latent capabilities on sensitive tasks, they remain at a global level and lack the means for fine-grained capability switching based on specific semantics or contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces the notation used throughout the paper, reviews the
two prevailing geometric theories of refusal, and formulates the
fine<span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>grained, mutually<span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>exclusive decomposition problem that motivates
<span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MEUV</span>. All implementation<span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>level details are deferred to
Sect. <a href="#S4" title="4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Notation and Refusal Probability</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let an instruction prompt be a token sequence
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
processed by an LLM
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks and hidden size <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\ell}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\ell}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
the residual stream of block <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and focus
on the end<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>of<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>instruction index <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{eoi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>eoi</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{eoi}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{ref}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{ref}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the logit of a reserved refusal token,
the differentiable refusal probability is defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Notation and Refusal Probability ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{ref}}(x)\;=\;\sigma\!\bigl(l_{\text{ref}}(x)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{ref}}(x)\;=\;\sigma\!\bigl(l_{\text{ref}}(x)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the logistic function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Directional ablation.</span> For a unit vector <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
directional ablation is defined as
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ablate}(h,v)=h-(h\!\cdot\!v)\,v," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Ablate</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ablate}(h,v)=h-(h\!\cdot\!v)\,v,</annotation></semantics></math>
a rank<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>1 intervention that removes the projection of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection ltx_pruned_first">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Limitations of Existing Theories</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Single<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>direction theory.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Arditi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> identify a single vector
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose magnitude predicts refusal, as given in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Single-direction theory. ‣ 3.2 Limitations of Existing Theories ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Refuse}(x)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;h(x)\!\cdot\!d_{\text{ref}}&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Refuse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Refuse}(x)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;h(x)\!\cdot\!d_{\text{ref}}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=h_{\ell}^{(t_{\mathrm{eoi}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>eoi</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=h_{\ell}^{(t_{\mathrm{eoi}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Ablating <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> reliably bypasses refusal, but the theory
treats all sensitive topics as a monolithic class.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refusal<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>cone theory.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequent work proposes a set
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{d_{1},\dots,d_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{d_{1},\dots,d_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that spans a refusal cone and models refusal as the maximum projection onto that set, as given in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Refusal-cone theory. ‣ 3.2 Limitations of Existing Theories ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Refuse}(x)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\max_{d\in C}h(x)\!\cdot\!d&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Refuse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Refuse}(x)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\max_{d\in C}h(x)\!\cdot\!d&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although the cone captures multiple linear mechanisms, Euclidean
orthogonality alone does not guarantee semantic separation; ablating
one direction may inadvertently unlock disallowed capabilities in others.
Neither Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Single-direction theory. ‣ 3.2 Limitations of Existing Theories ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) nor Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Refusal-cone theory. ‣ 3.2 Limitations of Existing Theories ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) therefore meets
the selective<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>access requirements of security<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>critical deployments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Fine<span id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>grained Decomposition Hypothesis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hypothesize that the canonical refusal vector
is a non<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>negative combination of
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> topic<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>aligned vectors, as given in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3 Fine-grained Decomposition Hypothesis ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{ref}}\;\approx\;\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{k}\,v_{k},\quad\alpha_{k}\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">≈</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{ref}}\;\approx\;\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{k}\,v_{k},\quad\alpha_{k}\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a distinct sensitive category
(<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">drug</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">terrorism</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">porn</span> …)
and can be used to unlock that category in isolation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Mutual<span id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Exclusivity Criterion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any prompt <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and topic vectors <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k\to j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k\to j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{\text{ref}}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ablate}(f(x),v_{k})\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Ablate</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{\text{ref}}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ablate}(f(x),v_{k})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(\emph{target bypass})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target bypass</em></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(\emph{target bypass})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{k\to j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{k\to j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{\text{ref}}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ablate}(f(x),v_{j})\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Ablate</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{\text{ref}}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ablate}(f(x),v_{j})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(\emph{cross bypass}), }j\neq k." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross bypass</em></mtext><mtext>), </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(\emph{cross bypass}), }j\neq k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A vector set <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-mutually<span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>exclusive if</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}(x)\leq 1-\tau\quad\text{and}\quad c_{k\to j}(x)\geq 1-\varepsilon\quad\forall x,\;k\neq j," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}(x)\leq 1-\tau\quad\text{and}\quad c_{k\to j}(x)\geq 1-\varepsilon\quad\forall x,\;k\neq j,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">with thresholds <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\tau&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\tau&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Approximate orthogonality
<math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\!\cdot\!v_{j}\!\approx\!0\;(i\!\neq\!j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\!\cdot\!v_{j}\!\approx\!0\;(i\!\neq\!j)</annotation></semantics></math>
encourages Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) but does not guarantee it; we therefore
regularize residual correlation via Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}=\sum_{i\neq j}(v_{i}\!\cdot\!v_{j})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ortho</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}=\sum_{i\neq j}(v_{i}\!\cdot\!v_{j})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Problem Statement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input.</span>
A corpus of prompts labeled by sensitive topic, plus a set of
general prompts for utility preservation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal.</span>
Learn</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a vector set <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the mutual<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>exclusivity condition
(<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an intent router <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> assigning unseen prompts to their topic
(or a fallback <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math>) such that standard benchmark performance
degrades by at most <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The next section introduces <span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MEUV</span>, a training procedure that
meets these objectives and empirically validates
the fine<span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>grained decomposition hypothesis on multiple models and two
languages.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>MEUV Framework</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection ltx_pruned_first">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Framework Overview</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12221v1/framework.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/322;" width="476" height="322" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Schematic diagram of the <span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MEUV</span> workflow. The framework automatically selects the matching unlocking vector based on the features of the user input prompt, and unlocks the corresponding capability by ablating this vector. The relevant capability of the LLMs is successfully unlocked only when the type of unlocking vector matches the type of input prompt.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Objective recap.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MEUV</span> to operationalize the goal formalized in
§<a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Problem Statement ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>: learning a vector set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and a router <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> that
satisfy the behavioral constraints in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Behavioral constraints. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
Concretely, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MEUV</span> minimizes the relaxed objective in
Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architecture.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MEUV is capable of generating a set of semantically exclusive unlocking vectors to enable fine-grained unlocking of LLMs capabilities. The workflow is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Framework Overview ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. To achieve this functionality, MEUV consists of three
cooperating modules:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unlock<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>vector learner</span> (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Multi-Task Loss Design ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>):
learns mutually<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>exclusive vectors by minimizing a five<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>term loss
capturing (i) target bypass, (ii) cross<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic safety,
(iii) utility retention, (iv) addition<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>side regularization, and
(v) approximate orthogonality.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contrastive intent router</span> (§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Contrastive Intent Router ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>):
a two<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>stage design—first trains a backbone encoder with
classification+supervised contrastive loss, then freezes it and
learns a projection <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with prototype (unlock<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>vector) matching to
map prompts to <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{1,\dots,K,\bot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,K,\bot\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference pipeline</span> (§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Inference Pipeline ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>):
group prompts by predicted topic; for each group, register
rank<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>1 ablation hooks with <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> across transformer blocks and
decode. Prompts routed to <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math> bypass any intervention.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training workflow.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Harmful, cross<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic harmful, and general prompts are sampled per topic.
For each sample we compute both baseline and intervened outputs to form
the loss terms in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Gradients update <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and the
router’s projection head; <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is periodically projected to enforce
near<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>orthogonality. Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Approximate Behavioral Guarantees under Mild Assumptions ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> provides behavioral
guarantees under mild assumptions, and §5 empirically validates the
design choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Formal Objective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> index hazardous topics (here <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>).
We denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the harmful prompts of topic <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{i}\ (i\neq k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{i}\ (i\neq k)</annotation></semantics></math> the harmful prompts of other topics, and by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> the harmless/general prompts.
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be the base LLM, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{abl}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{abl}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{add}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>add</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{add}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the model after ablating or adding vector <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to input <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> be the illicit reference answer (used to measure unlocking) and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> a fixed refusal template.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the self-supervised ablation CE gap, the supervised ablation CE, and the addition CE as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), and Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), respectively, as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f(x),y\bigr)-\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x,v),y\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.412em;" width="1.412em"><mi>CE</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.412em;" width="1.412em"><mi>CE</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f(x),y\bigr)-\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x,v),y\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x{+}y,v),y\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.412em;" width="1.412em"><mi>CE</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x{+}y,v),y\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{CE}_{\text{add}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>add</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{CE}_{\text{add}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{add}}(x,v),y_{\text{ref}}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.412em;" width="1.412em"><mi>CE</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>add</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{CE}\!\bigl(f_{\text{add}}(x,v),y_{\text{ref}}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CE}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence cross<span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>entropy with ignore tokens, and the refusal probability defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Notation and Refusal Probability ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) can equivalently be represented using cross-entropy loss with a fixed refusal token; <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{abl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{abl}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the output logits of the LLMs obtained by applying directional ablation intervention mentioned in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) at appropriate transformer blocks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Behavioral constraints.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We want (i) target bypass, (ii) cross<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic safety, and (iii) utility preservation as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Behavioral constraints. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(BYPASS)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(BYPASS)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(BYPASS)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\;\geq\;\tau," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\;\geq\;\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{H}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(CROSS)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(CROSS)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(CROSS)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})\;\leq\;\delta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})\;\leq\;\delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{H}_{i},\;j\neq i," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{H}_{i},\;j\neq i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(UTILITY)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(UTILITY)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(UTILITY)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\,\|\,f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\;\leq\;\zeta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\,\|\,f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\;\leq\;\zeta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xb.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{G}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall\,x\in\mathcal{G},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> enforcing sufficient unlocking, a small <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> limiting spill, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> bounding harmless drift.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Smooth relaxation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We upper<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>bound violations via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{softplus}(z)=\log(1+e^{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>z</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{softplus}(z)=\log(1+e^{z})</annotation></semantics></math> and add an orthogonality penalty as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(V)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(V)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="6" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{by}}\!\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}}\!\!\Bigl[(1-\beta)\,\operatorname{softplus}\bigl(\tau-\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)+\beta\,\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\Bigr]}_{\text{(1) Target bypass}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>by</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(1) Target bypass</mtext></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{by}}\!\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}}\!\!\Bigl[(1-\beta)\,\operatorname{softplus}\bigl(\tau-\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)+\beta\,\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\Bigr]}_{\text{(1) Target bypass}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{cr}}\sum_{i\neq j}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{i}}\operatorname{softplus}\bigl(\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})-\delta\bigr)}_{\text{(2) Cross-topic safety}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>cr</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>softplus</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(2) Cross-topic safety</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{cr}}\sum_{i\neq j}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{i}}\operatorname{softplus}\bigl(\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})-\delta\bigr)}_{\text{(2) Cross-topic safety}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{addCE}}\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{G}}\operatorname{CE}_{\text{add}}(x,v_{k})}_{\text{(3) Addition CE on harmless data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>addCE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>add</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(3) Addition CE on harmless data</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{addCE}}\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{G}}\operatorname{CE}_{\text{add}}(x,v_{k})}_{\text{(3) Addition CE on harmless data}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{ut}}\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{G}}\operatorname{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\,\|\,f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)}_{\text{(4) Utility retention (ablation branch)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ut</mtext></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(4) Utility retention (ablation branch)</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{ut}}\sum_{k}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{G}}\operatorname{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\,\|\,f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)}_{\text{(4) Utility retention (ablation branch)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xe" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xe.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{ortho}}\;\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{(5) Orthogonality regularizer}}\;+\;\lambda_{\text{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>ortho</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(5) Orthogonality regularizer</mtext></munder></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>proto</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\lambda_{\text{ortho}}\;\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}}_{\text{(5) Orthogonality regularizer}}\;+\;\lambda_{\text{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=[v_{1};\dots;v_{K}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=[v_{1};\dots;v_{K}]</annotation></semantics></math> stacks all vectors, the hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> balances the self-supervised CE gap with the supervised ablation CE, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Frobenius norm, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>proto</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an optional prototype/InfoNCE auxiliary loss to stabilize the prompt–vector assignment. The orthogonality regularizer term <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> equivalently captures pairwise orthogonality via Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Minimizing (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is a smooth Lagrangian relaxation of the behavioral constraints in (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Behavioral constraints. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). The margins <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> instantiate the thresholds on bypass and spill in practice, while <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> controls harmless drift. (If one prefers the alternative sign convention <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{abl}}\leq-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{abl}}\leq-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to reparametrize <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and flip the arguments of the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">softplus</span>.)</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Multi<span id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Task Loss Design</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The relaxed objective in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) decomposes into five
computational terms. Below we summarize their functional roles; detailed
definitions are given in Eqs. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target bypass (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{by}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">by</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{by}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
Softplus<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau-\Delta_{\text{abl}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau-\Delta_{\text{abl}})</annotation></semantics></math> enforces a minimum ablation gap on
in<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic harmful prompts (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)), guaranteeing the
bypass margin. Meanwhile, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math> leverages supervised learning to encourage the LLMs to generate content that was originally prohibited, following the ablation intervention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic safety (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">cr</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cr}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
Softplus<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\text{abl}}-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\text{abl}}-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> penalizes any unintended gap
improvement when a foreign vector is applied (third line of
Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Addition CE on harmless data (<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{add}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">add</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{add}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) requires that adding a vector to benign prompts
still prefers the refusal template, preventing bidirectional misuse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility retention (<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">ut</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ut}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
KL<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(f\|f_{\text{abl}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f\|f_{\text{abl}})</annotation></semantics></math> bounds distributional drift on general
prompts (third constraint in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Behavioral constraints. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orthogonality regularizer (<math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">ortho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
<math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> promotes geometric independence among vectors and
tightens the cross<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>topic bound (last term in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Optionally, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>proto</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{proto}}</annotation></semantics></math> aligns the router’s projected
space with <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> via a prototype/contrastive objective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection ltx_pruned_first">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Contrastive Intent Router</h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlock vectors <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the LLM’s residual space,
whose basis and scale differ from those of a semantic encoder.
To bridge these spaces, the router introduces a learnable linear
projection <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W:\mathbb{R}^{d_{e}}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W:\mathbb{R}^{d_{e}}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps
encoder embeddings into the unlock<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>vector space. We then use the unlock
vectors themselves as class prototypes, turning routing into a prototype
selection problem in the aligned space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Two<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>stage optimization.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The router <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g:\mathcal{X}\!\to\!\{1,\dots,K,\bot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathcal{X}\!\to\!\{1,\dots,K,\bot\}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained in two
decoupled stages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage I (encoder training).</span>
A sentence encoder <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on topic<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>labeled prompts with a
hybrid objective as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Two-stage optimization. ‣ 4.4 Contrastive Intent Router ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{enc}}=\beta_{\text{ce}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}+\beta_{\text{sup}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{SupCon}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>enc</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>ce</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SupCon</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{enc}}=\beta_{\text{ce}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}+\beta_{\text{sup}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{SupCon}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">combining cross<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>entropy classification and supervised contrastive loss to
obtain a semantically structured embedding space <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II (projection &amp; routing).</span>
During unlock<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>vector learning, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is frozen. We optimize only the
projection <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and use <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> as the prototype set.
Let <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}(x)=W\,e(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}(x)=W\,e(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and define cosine scores in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Two-stage optimization. ‣ 4.4 Contrastive Intent Router ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{s}_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{s}_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\tilde{z}(x)^{\top}v_{k}}{\tilde{z}(x)\,v_{k}}\,\frac{1}{\tau_{r}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\tilde{z}(x)^{\top}v_{k}}{\tilde{z}(x)\,v_{k}}\,\frac{1}{\tau_{r}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E14Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\exp\bigl(\tilde{s}_{k}(x)\bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\exp\bigl(\tilde{s}_{j}(x)\bigr)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\exp\bigl(\tilde{s}_{k}(x)\bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\exp\bigl(\tilde{s}_{j}(x)\bigr)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Routing is trained with an InfoNCE objective over these scores as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In Two-stage optimization. ‣ 4.4 Contrastive Intent Router ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{router}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\log s_{\ell_{b}}(x_{b})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>router</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{router}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\log s_{\ell_{b}}(x_{b}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>truth topic index.
Benign prompts are included as negatives; prompts judged
benign form a fallback class <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math> that bypasses vector intervention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision rule.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference time, decisions are made according to Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Decision rule. ‣ 4.4 Contrastive Intent Router ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=\begin{cases}\arg\max_{k\in\{1,\dots,K\}}s_{k}(x),&amp;\shortstack[l]{\text{if a sensitive topic}\\
 \text{is detected}},\\[2.0pt]
\bot,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_left"> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text">if a sensitive topic</span></span> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text">is detected</span></span> 
</span></mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=\begin{cases}\arg\max_{k\in\{1,\dots,K\}}s_{k}(x),&amp;\shortstack[l]{\text{if a sensitive topic}\\
 \text{is detected}},\\[2.0pt]
\bot,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, only prompts mapped to a sensitive topic receive the corresponding directional ablation. This semantic gate precedes all mechanistic interventions and constitutes the first layer of MEUV’s safety barrier.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Inference Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After training the unlock vectors <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and the contrastive intent router <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, inference in MEUV follows a structured pipeline consisting of semantic routing, batch-wise grouping, directional intervention, and decoding steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, given a set of input prompts <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the router <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> first assigns each prompt to a sensitive topic category or a fallback category (<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math>). Prompts categorized under sensitive topics are subsequently grouped into distinct batches according to their assigned categories, while those classified as benign (<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math>) bypass any directional intervention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inference procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4.5 Inference Pipeline ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>MEUV Inference Pipeline</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Prompt set <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, unlock vectors <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, intent router <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">Obtain topic assignments: <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}\leftarrow{x\in\mathcal{X}:g(x)=k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}\leftarrow{x\in\mathcal{X}:g(x)=k}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">topic <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{1,\dots,K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{1,\dots,K}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Register layer-wise directional ablation hooks. </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\textsc{GetAllDirectionAblationHooks}(v_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">GetAllDirectionAblationHooks</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\leftarrow\textsc{GetAllDirectionAblationHooks}(v_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Generate outputs for batch: </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k}\leftarrow\textsc{Generate}(\mathcal{X}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Generate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k}\leftarrow\textsc{Generate}(\mathcal{X}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Remove hooks immediately after decoding. </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{RemoveHooks}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">RemoveHooks</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{RemoveHooks}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Generate outputs without intervention for benign prompts. </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\bot}\leftarrow\textsc{NativeGenerateOrRefuse}(\mathcal{X}_{\bot})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">NativeGenerateOrRefuse</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\bot}\leftarrow\textsc{NativeGenerateOrRefuse}(\mathcal{X}_{\bot})</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Aggregated outputs. </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\leftarrow\bigcup_{k}Y_{k}\cup Y_{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\leftarrow\bigcup_{k}Y_{k}\cup Y_{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math>;


</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Directional Ablation Mechanism.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For prompts assigned to sensitive topics, MEUV applies a rank-1 directional ablation intervention across the model’s residual streams. Hooks are applied both as pre-forward and post-forward hooks across the model’s attention and MLP modules, thereby consistently intervening on the internal representations before decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Complexity.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of non-empty prompt buckets categorized as sensitive. Each non-empty category requires a separate forward pass, incurring an additional constant-time rank-1 projection operation at each transformer layer. Consequently, MEUV’s memory footprint and parameter count remain identical to the original model, although the total computational cost scales linearly with <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. In practical deployments, <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of sensitive categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Security Guarantees.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MEUV’s inference procedure ensures robust safeguards via a two-tiered mechanism:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic gate</span>: The intent router <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> filters prompts to apply interventions only when explicitly necessary. Benign or irrelevant prompts are directly subjected to the native model policy without modification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mechanistic gate</span>: For prompts identified as sensitive, exactly one unlock vector per category is applied, adhering strictly to the mutual-exclusivity conditions defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.4 Mutual-Exclusivity Criterion ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Mis-routed prompts are inherently constrained by the cross-topic safety bounds ensured during optimization.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, MEUV guarantees selective and controlled unlocking of sensitive capabilities, effectively maintaining security properties in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Approximate Behavioral Guarantees under Mild Assumptions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our target bypass loss function combines two complementary terms: a well-established supervised ablation loss (<math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CE</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CE}_{\text{abl}}</annotation></semantics></math>), previously validated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and a novel self-supervised ablation gap measure (<math id="S4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{abl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{abl}}</annotation></semantics></math>), which we introduce here. Given that the effectiveness and theoretical validity of the supervised term have already been established, we focus our theoretical analysis specifically on our newly proposed self-supervised term <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{abl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{abl}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to clarify its approximate guarantees and behavior under mild assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumptions.</span>
(A1) Each per-sample <span id="S4.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">softplus</span> term in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Smooth relaxation. ‣ 4.2 Formal Objective ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
(A2) The CE gap is monotone and <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{ce}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>ce</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{ce}}</annotation></semantics></math>–Lipschitz w.r.t. the first-token refusal probability (or its logit).
(A3) (optional) <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>–Lipschitz w.r.t. the hidden state <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Approximate Mutual<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>Exclusivity)<span id="Thmproposition1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{by}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">by</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{by}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, 
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cr}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">cr</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cr}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, 
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ut}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ut</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{x}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ut}}(x)\!\leq\!\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,  and
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}\!\leq\!\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ortho</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ortho}}\!\leq\!\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any topic <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and prompt <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequalities collected in
Eq. (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In Proposition 1 (Approximate Mutual-Exclusivity). ‣ 4.6 Approximate Behavioral Guarantees under Mild Assumptions ‣ 4 MEUV Framework ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) hold:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(BYPASS)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(BYPASS)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(BYPASS)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\geq\tau-\eta_{\text{by}}(\epsilon)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">by</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\geq\tau-\eta_{\text{by}}(\epsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{H}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(CROSS)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(CROSS)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(CROSS)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\leq\delta+\eta_{\text{cr}}(\epsilon)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">cr</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\leq\delta+\eta_{\text{cr}}(\epsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{H}_{j},\ j\neq k," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{H}_{j},\ j\neq k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(UTILITY)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(UTILITY)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(UTILITY)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\|f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\leq\zeta+\eta_{\text{ut}}(\epsilon)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">abl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ut</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{KL}\!\bigl(f(x)\|f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\leq\zeta+\eta_{\text{ut}}(\epsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{G}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\in\mathcal{G},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\text{by}},\eta_{\text{cr}},\eta_{\text{ut}}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">by</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">cr</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ut</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\text{by}},\eta_{\text{cr}},\eta_{\text{ut}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <span id="Thmproposition1.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">softplus</span>, one can take
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(\epsilon)=\operatorname{softplus}^{-1}(\epsilon)=\log(e^{\epsilon}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>softplus</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\epsilon)=\operatorname{softplus}^{-1}(\epsilon)=\log(e^{\epsilon}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\bullet}(\epsilon)\leq C\,\eta(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\bullet}(\epsilon)\leq C\,\eta(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Orthogonality.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the ablation operator <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is (approximately) an orthogonal projector (<math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|P_{v}\|_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|P_{v}\|_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>)
and (A3) holds, then whenever <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{2}\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|VV^{\top}-I\|_{2}\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})\big|\leq L_{h}\,\eta\,\|h(x)\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|\Delta_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{j})\big|\leq L_{h}\,\eta\,\|h(x)\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{H}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{H}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>;
thus a small spectral deviation <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> alone limits cross-topic leakage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Router robustness.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be the router mis-route rate. A coarse worst-case leakage bound is
<math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho+\eta_{\text{cr}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mtext>cr</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho+\eta_{\text{cr}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>; hence the safety budget is respected whenever <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing open-source safety corpora annotate toxicity at only a single granularity level, and therefore cannot support experiments requiring topic-specific unlocking. To obtain the necessary fine<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>grained signals,
we synthesize a bilingual corpus (zh-cn, en) covering three hazardous topics
— <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">drugs</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">terrorism</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">porn</span> — plus a harmless
reference class. Prompts and reference completions are generated using
GPT<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>3.5 and then manually verified for topical purity. The feature distributions of the various types of data are illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1.1 Datasets ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments and Analysis ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For every language-specific dataset, we create three splits:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Training</em>. The training split contains 1,215 harmful–harmless sentence pairs. On the harmful side, the three toxicity sub-categories—drugs, terrorism, and porn—are represented in equal proportions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Validation / Test</em>. The development (320 instances) and test (460 instances) splits consist of single sentences (no pairing) and are uniformly stratified across the four labels.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12221v1/data_tsne.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/699;" width="476" height="699" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Feature distribution visualization of the experimental training data.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Evaluation Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we introduce two metrics to separately evaluate the unlocking success rate and the specificity of unlocking for MEUV.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>success rate (ASR).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each hazardous topic, we ask the LLMs to answer the corresponding
harmful prompt.
A run is deemed successful if the generated response does
not contain the refusal information <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E18" title="In Attack-success rate (ASR). ‣ 5.1.2 Evaluation Metrics. ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments and Analysis ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{ASR}(v_{k})=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}_{k}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}}\mathbbm{1}\!\Bigl[\,y_{\text{ref}}\not\subset\textsc{Generate}\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\Bigr]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ASR</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>⊄</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Generate</mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>abl</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ASR}(v_{k})=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}_{k}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}_{k}}\mathbbm{1}\!\Bigl[\,y_{\text{ref}}\not\subset\textsc{Generate}\bigl(f_{\text{abl}}(x,v_{k})\bigr)\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{1}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.
ASR therefore measures the proportion of harmful prompts whose directional
ablation suppresses refusal in the final text, making it the key
metric for unlock effectiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unlock<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Specificity Gap (USG).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify how selectively a vector targets its own hazardous
topic while leaving others unaffected, we construct an
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> score matrix
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=[m_{ij}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=[m_{ij}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}=\operatorname{ASR}(v_{j}\!\mid\!\mathcal{H}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ASR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}=\operatorname{ASR}(v_{j}\!\mid\!\mathcal{H}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Perfect specificity would yield the identity matrix <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. This is shown in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Unlock-Specificity Gap (USG). ‣ 5.1.2 Evaluation Metrics. ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments and Analysis ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{USG}(M)=1-\frac{\displaystyle\iota\sum_{i=1}^{N}\lvert 1-m_{ii}\rvert^{p}+\;\vartheta\!\!\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}i,j=1\\
i\neq j\end{subarray}}^{N}\lvert m_{ij}\rvert^{p}}{\iota\,N+\vartheta\,N(N-1)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>USG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.251em;" width="0.251em"><mi>ϑ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{USG}(M)=1-\frac{\displaystyle\iota\sum_{i=1}^{N}\lvert 1-m_{ii}\rvert^{p}+\;\vartheta\!\!\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}i,j=1\\
i\neq j\end{subarray}}^{N}\lvert m_{ij}\rvert^{p}}{\iota\,N+\vartheta\,N(N-1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota,\vartheta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϑ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota,\vartheta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> weight the diagonal and off<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>diagonal deviations
(default <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota=\vartheta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota=\vartheta=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> chooses an
L<sup id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup">p</sup> penalty.
USG is normalized to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{USG}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>USG</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{USG}=1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=I</annotation></semantics></math> (ideal topic exclusivity) and
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{USG}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>USG</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{USG}=0</annotation></semantics></math> when all diagonals are 0 and off<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>diagonals are 1
(maximum spill<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>over).
A higher USG therefore indicates stronger vector–task specificity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refusal Metric (RM).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our training process, we also employed the Refusal Metric (RM) as a rapid evaluation indicator. The RM solely examines whether the first token generated by the LLMs falls within the set of refusal prefixes, as shown in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Refusal Metric (RM). ‣ 5.1.2 Evaluation Metrics. ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments and Analysis ‣ MEUV: Achieving Fine-Grained Capability Activation in Large Language Models via Mutually Exclusive Unlock Vectors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{first}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>first</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{first}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of token IDs that begin any
author<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>defined refusal prefix, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
softmax probability assigned to token <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as the first decoded
token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RM}=\log\!\Biggl(\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{first}}}p_{t}}{1-\displaystyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}}p_{t}}\Biggr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RM</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>first</mtext></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RM}=\log\!\Biggl(\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{first}}}p_{t}}{1-\displaystyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}}p_{t}}\Biggr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection ltx_pruned_first">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3 </span>Models and Baselines</h4>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Backbone LLMs.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply MEUV to three representative open–source models:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.6em;">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>2-2B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>: compact yet competitive on general benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaMA<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>3<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>: the prevailing research reference for English<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>centric evaluation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen<span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>: tuned for Chinese and bilingual usage.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The publicly released instruction<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuned checkpoints are used without any
additional gradient fine<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuning; all interventions are performed by
residual<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>space editing alone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark against the single<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>vector method of Arditi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
RD identifies a global refusal direction inside the model and then
either
(i) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ablation</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub">ablation</sub></span>): removes the projection onto that
vector, or
(ii) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">activation addition</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_sub">addition</sub></span>): adds a scaled copy of
the vector to the residual stream.
The ablation variant is acknowledged as the most stable white<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>box
unlocking technique to date, while the addition variant is reported by
the authors to further loosen refusal in some cases.
Because RD already delivers strong and repeatable performance, we regard
it as a sufficient baseline; introducing weaker or less deterministic
methods would not sharpen the comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both MEUV and RD edit the same hidden location—the final residual stream
of every transformer block.
MEUV always operates in the ablation mode, consistent with its
design (directional suppression of the safety component).
RD results are reported for <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both</span> modes (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub">ablation</sub></span>,
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RD<sub id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub">addition</sub></span>) to exhaust its published variants.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.4 </span>Hyper<span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>parameters and Environment</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because each unlock vector has the same dimensionality as the model’s
hidden state, its representational capacity is limited; in practice we
observe rapid convergence. All experiments therefore use a single
epoch over the training split. In addition, we employ a <span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">topic<span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>
wise early<span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>stopping</span> rule: as soon as the validation
RM for topic <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is no higher than the
corresponding RD score, the vector <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is frozen and excluded from
subsequent updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, the prompt encoder for English tasks was implemented based on all-mpnet-base-v2, while for Chinese tasks, bge-base-zh-v1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> was used as the encoder backbone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our code is implemented in <span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PyTorch</span> 2.5.1 and relies on the
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transformers</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">accelerate</span> toolkits for mixed<span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>precision
training. All experiments are conducted on a single NVIDIA A800 GPU.
Excluding periodic validation, training a complete set of unlock vectors
for one framework takes approximately 30 minutes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Unlocking performance comparison of MEUV and RD on three LLMs (English Harmful dataset). The best-performing results are emphasized in <span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
